.TH SKELL 8 "2026-07-17" "Kiro" "Kiro System Tools"
.SH NAME
skell \- restore default configuration from /etc/skel, backing up only the configs it overwrites
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B skell
[\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR]
[\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR]
[\fB\-d\fR|\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B skell
is the short alias for
.BR kiro-skell (8).
It copies the system skeleton directory
.RI ( /etc/skel )
into the current user's home directory, bringing new default configuration
files into use without permanently losing existing customisations.
.PP
New or updated defaults for the shipped desktops, tiling window managers and
applications land in
.I /etc/skel
as the distribution evolves.
.B skell
is the tool that pulls those defaults into an already-installed account, which
otherwise keeps whatever configuration it was created with.
.SS Scoped backup
Before copying, and this is the point of the tool,
.B skell
backs up
.I only
the
.I ~/.config
entries that
.I /etc/skel/.config
is about to overwrite \(em not the whole
.IR ~/.config ,
which can hold gigabytes of browser and application cache. For each top-level
entry present in
.I /etc/skel/.config
that also exists under
.IR ~/.config ,
that entry is copied into a timestamped backup at
.IP
.I ~/.config-backup-YYYY.MM.DD-HH.MM.SS/.config/
.PP
so the previous state of the overwritten configs can be restored by hand if a
new default is not to your liking. Entries in
.I ~/.config
that
.I /etc/skel
does not touch are left completely alone and are
.I not
copied \(em that is what keeps the operation fast.
.PP
For a full, unconditional backup of the entire
.I ~/.config
tree before overwriting, use
.BR kiro-skell-all (8)
instead.
.SS Overwrite step
After the scoped backup, the copy itself is unconditional: the complete
contents of
.I /etc/skel
(dotfiles and directories alike) are copied over the top of the home directory
with
.RB ' "cp -r /etc/skel/. $HOME" '.
Existing files with the same path are overwritten. This is intentional \(em
.B skell
restores the shipped defaults.
.SS Interaction and safety
In interactive mode (no
.BR \-\-dry\-run )
.B skell
prints a summary of what it is about to do and prompts for a single
yes/no confirmation before any file is touched. Answering anything other than
.B y
or
.B Y
aborts with nothing changed.
.PP
In
.B \-\-dry\-run
mode the confirmation prompt is skipped and every action \(em both the backup
and the overwrite \(em is printed rather than performed, so you can preview the
exact commands first.
.PP
Because it needs no graphical session,
.B skell
is the standard recovery step when a tiling window manager will not start after
a broken configuration change: switch to a TTY, run
.BR skell ,
and log back in with the shipped defaults restored.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a help message and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print version information (the owning pacman package and version, when
installed that way) and exit.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-dry\-run
Show what would be executed without making any changes. Implies non-interactive
operation \(em the confirmation prompt is skipped.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/skel/
Source skeleton directory; its entire contents are copied into the home
directory.
.TP
.I ~/.config/
The entries whose names also appear under
.I /etc/skel/.config
are backed up before being overwritten; all other entries are untouched.
.TP
.I ~/.config-backup-YYYY.MM.DD-HH.MM.SS/
Timestamped backup directory created for the overwritten
.I ~/.config
entries.
.SH EXAMPLES
Preview exactly what would be copied and backed up, changing nothing:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
skell --dry-run
.EE
.RE
.PP
Restore the shipped defaults interactively (prompts before touching anything):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
skell
.EE
.RE
.PP
Recover a crashing tiling window manager from a TTY:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
# switch to a text console with Ctrl+Alt+F3, log in, then:
skell
.EE
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, or the user declined at the confirmation prompt (nothing changed).
.TP
.B 1
An unknown option was supplied.
.SH NOTES
.B skell
is a symlink to
.BR kiro-skell (8);
the two are the same program. All of its user-facing output derives from the
name it is invoked as, so running it as
.B skell
prints
.B skell
throughout. The script is self-contained and does not depend on
.IR /usr/local/lib/kiro-common.sh ,
so a verbatim copy runs unchanged on any distribution.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kiro-skell (8),
.BR kiro-skell-all (8),
.BR cp (1)
